\label{sec:theory}

The primary objective of this theoretical framework is to establish a mathematically rigorous dynamical-systems perspective on regime transitions within the nocturnal stable boundary layer (SBL). Modeling stably stratified atmospheric boundary layers remains a major challenge because of the multiscale coupling between rapid turbulent dissipation and the comparatively slow evolution of surface cooling and mesoscale forcing. Under sufficiently strong surface stratification, the SBL frequently undergoes an abrupt collapse of turbulence, transitioning from an actively turbulent state to a weakly turbulent or nearly laminar regime that becomes partially or fully decoupled from the surface.

To analyze these transitions without relying on empirical stability functions, we formulate the boundary layer as a singularly perturbed dynamical system governed by Geometric Singular Perturbation Theory (GSPT). This formulation exploits the natural separation between fast turbulent adjustment and slower atmospheric evolution:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Fast subsystem:} The relaxation of turbulent kinetic energy ($e$), governed primarily by local shear production, buoyant destruction, and viscous dissipation, operating on characteristic turbulent timescales of approximately $10^{1}\text{--}10^{2}\,\mathrm{s}$.
    \item \textbf{Slow subsystem:} The thermodynamic and dynamical evolution of the background state, represented by the horizontal momentum components ($U,V$) and the surface skin temperature ($T_s$), which evolve under geostrophic forcing, Coriolis rotation, radiative cooling, and surface energy exchange on timescales of approximately $10^{3}\text{--}10^{4}\,\mathrm{s}$.
\end{itemize}

In the singular limit as the timescale-separation parameter satisfies $\varepsilon \to 0$, the fast dynamics rapidly relax onto an equilibrium manifold known as the critical manifold, $\mathcal{S}_0$. The geometry of $\mathcal{S}_0$ determines the admissible quasi-equilibrium turbulent states. Fold singularities correspond to losses of normal hyperbolicity, where attracting and repelling branches merge and the reduced slow dynamics cease to be well defined. These singularities organize rapid transitions between turbulent and weakly turbulent regimes through the fast subsystem. For physically relevant values satisfying $0 < \varepsilon \ll 1$, Fenichel's persistence theorem guarantees the existence of a nearby perturbed invariant slow manifold, $\mathcal{S}_\varepsilon$, away from fold singularities. The geometry of $\mathcal{S}_\varepsilon$ subsequently governs hysteresis, delayed transitions, and regime memory observed within the nocturnal boundary layer.

To ensure that this geometric framework transfers consistently to single-column model (SCM) implementations, we introduce a smooth regularization of the turbulent state variables. A positive background turbulent kinetic energy floor ($\delta$), together with a continuously differentiable gating function ($\psi$), eliminates derivative singularities as turbulence approaches zero while preserving forward invariance of the physically admissible state space. Importantly, this regularization preserves the underlying fast--slow geometric structure required by GSPT while preventing numerical pathologies associated with turbulence collapse. Consequently, the abstract bifurcation geometry of the slow manifold can be translated directly into physically realizable parameterizations constrained by observations, such as the CASES-99 field campaign, thereby providing a rigorous foundation for the governing equations developed in the following sections.
